**Overall 8/10, unchanged from R9.** Long Cat Out! meets the solid top-100 benchmark as a design. R10 brings retention and virality up from 7 to 8. The best moment is still the same one: making a risky bench decision and watching it pay off in a chain.

| Dimension | R9 | R10 | Assessment |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| Presentation | 8 | **8** | The café, street, and puzzle board feel like one world. Cat Street still crowds the shop façades with controls while leaving a large stretch of empty road. |
| Appeal | 8 | **8** | The sleeping bench cats and postcard portraits sell the fantasy. At rest, the straight cats still read as patterned tubes with expressive faces. |
| Juice | 8 | **8** | The paws-first leap has a recognizable rhythm, and the chain count makes each landing legible. The carrier and cushions could react more visibly to contact. |
| Fun | 8 | **8** | Choosing which cat can afford to wait gives the puzzle real stakes. The free Take back makes a full-bench loss recoverable, but the lesson still relies on reading the sheet. |
| Retention | 7 | **8** | Finished rooms point to the next build. Resident jobs give today a goal, and the album and Daily record give repeat visits a lasting payoff. A nearly complete album page can still stall for weeks. |
| Virality | 7 | **8** | A single link opens the exact board, and the named thread and counter-dare give the recipient a reason to reply. The clip still asks viewers to type the URL if it arrives without a caption. |
| **Overall** | **8** | **8** | The progression and sharing loops now match the quality of the core puzzle. |

### Most important remaining fixes

1. **Presentation.** At 375 × 667, collapse the completed-floor rail into a single house picker on Cat Street. Move shop names into a dedicated row below the façades and use the recovered road space to keep both goal tiles and Play visible.
2. **Appeal.** Start with the orange tabby. Widen the shoulders under the head, round the belly, taper the rump, and wrap the stripes around those contours. Check it at board size on both straight cats and tight bends before rolling it out to other breeds.
3. **Juice.** Give each landing a visible contact beat. Hold the paws briefly on the carrier lip, compress the body into the opening, and let the carrier settle with a small dip. Rebound each cushion as its cat launches during a chain. Fit these reactions into the current timing and preserve overlapping taps.
4. **Fun.** Turn the first full-bench loss into a playable lesson. Pulse the needed carrier or its queue badge and show how many departures away it is. After **Take back**, briefly mark the restored free cushion and one cat that can board immediately, where available. Check whether fresh players change their next choice without a prompt.
5. **Retention.** Shorten the wait for the first framed print. Make one page completable within the first four event weeks, let players use their weekly archive claim on any already-released postcard, and show the exact board or release date in each missing slot.
6. **Virality.** Add a scannable QR for the public HTTPS dare URL to the end card and fallback PNG. Verify send → received message → first move → reply on iPhone/iMessage, iPhone/WhatsApp, and Android/WhatsApp, including a clip stripped of its caption. Carry a compact move log in reply URLs and replay it locally before crediting chain or clean-sweep scores in Friend Results.

I scored from the supplied captures, notes, plan, and repo checks. The economy simulation, web, audio, and dare checks passed. The core check found zero solver misses across 600 levels, but exceeded its 60 ms worst-case generation budget on both runs. Physical-phone delivery and player retention remain unmeasured, so the retention and virality scores reflect design quality. [R10 notes](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/qa/r9-notes.md>) · [R10 plan](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/qa/sol-r10-plan.md>)
